WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400 align:start position:0%
When did the first animals appear on
Earth? Not bacteria or viruses, living,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.000 align:start position:0%
breathing, moving, multi-celled
creatures. We know animals were around

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:13.400 align:start position:0%
at least 574 million years ago, which is
about twice as long ago as dinosaurs

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.400 align:start position:0%
because we found these fossils of sea
animals, which are the oldest we've

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:20.100 align:start position:0%
found of any animal. But these animals
didn't just out of nowhere. They must

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.800 align:start position:0%
have evolved from something else. So
scientists looked at the rate of [music]

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:29.200 align:start position:0%
genetic mutations in a lineage to
basically rewind evolution, working

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:32.900 align:start position:0%
backward to a maximum age, a layer of
rock that we know [music]

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:36.100 align:start position:0%
can support animal fossils, but there
aren't any. After they did this to two

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:41.100 align:start position:0%
different groups of fossils, they found
that animals were probably around 200

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.800 align:start position:0%
million years earlier than we thought,
meaning they could have been around when

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:51.300 align:start position:0%
Earth was completely covered in ice,
scientifically known as snowball Earth.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.300 align:start position:0%
To see snowball Earth, subscribe.

